the fire swept through the top floors of  this four-story Factory nestled in the  heart of a West End residential area  residents say hundreds of people lived  and worked here making plastic bags and  bottles 23-year old Simon was lucky to  escape with her family we were asleep  when we heard people shouting about the  fire we saw people from the second floor  running down we were asleep on the first  almost everyone ran out only those who  were fast asleep died or were injured  there were small children on the first  floor and we managed to get them all out  but the fire was so terrible on the  second and third floor that we could not  reach it the fire was really bad  struggling to reach those trapped inside  the building because of the narrow and  crowded streets emergency workers were  forced to carry the injured two  ambulances waiting nearby there are  hundreds of factories like these which  employ thousands of people illegally  running in residential areas now this is  like a tinderbox waiting for a tragedy  of this sort to happen it took 25 fire  trucks and over a hundred and fifty men  to put the blaze out desperate and  distraught relatives could only wait  outside the hospital to identify the  dead most of those working here were  laborers from the state of Bihar in the  north-east of india  Bergman's had it they put all those who  were taken out from the building on fire  in an ambulance and bought them here we  were about 30 people from my village  affected in this incident but have only  been able to see some of them  an investigation into the fire is now  under way but tragedies like these have  become all too frequent as the focus now  turns to safety standards in the  nation's capital  never Lazarus Sky News Delhi
 

